    "collected_at": "2026-11-08T12:19:10.552600+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/novitec-tuned-lamborghini-temerario-looks-like-a-chic-gotham-city-prop-277999.html",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/novitec-tuned-lamborghini-temerario-looks-like-a-chic-gotham-city-prop-277999.html",
    "origin_publisher": "autoevolution",
    "published_at": "2026-11-08T11:40:01+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-08T11:40:01+0000",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-08T12:26:39.209249+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-08T12:26:39.209249+00:00",
    "body": "EICMA 2026 • 2027 Suzuki SV-7S Unveiled at the EICMA Show in Italy: I’m Suddenly More Nostalgic Than Ever\nNovitec-Tuned Lamborghini Temerario Looks Like a Chic Gotham City Prop\n20 photos\nPhoto: Instagram | novitecgroup\nDo all exotic models with a black overall look seem like they could easily be a Gotham City prop? Well, most of them do, and even if that may sound like a cliché, it is what it is. And in this case, it is a tuned Lamborghini Temerario, as mentioned in the title.\nSigned by Novitec and shared on social media not long ago, this machine easily stands out from the crowd, and proves that blue-blooded rides do not need lively paint finishes in order to make a statement, because a fully-black example can also do that. Well, an almost fully black one, because the brake calipers were actually painted purple in order to tie the supercar’s exterior to the cockpit.\nThat’s because this modded Lamborghini Temerario has quite a few purple touches on the inside, which can be seen on the seats, on the sides of the center console, some parts of the dashboard, and the door cards. White piping and contrast stitching can also be seen inside this machine. The dominating color is black, and it is a mix of leather and Alcantara upholstery that was wrapped around most touchable surfaces.\nThe top parts of the dashboard panel, the pillars, headliner, steering wheel, and other components were wrapped in Alcantara. The usual amount of carbon fiber trim is also on deck. The Raging Bull’s logo was embroidered into the integrated headrests, Lamborghini’s name adorns the dashboard in front of the passenger, the Temerario name greets users upon opening the doors, and the OEM emblem is still found on the steering wheel.\nPhoto: Instagram | novitecgroup Those who are unfamiliar with the design of the Lamborghini Temerario might be tempted to say that things such as the apron, the front hood, the side skirts, ducktail spoiler-like element at the rear, diffuser, and probably other parts on the outside have come from the aftermarket world. But they haven’t, because Novitec hasn’t signed them. These components are actually the OEM ones, just like the headlights, taillights, and so on.\nThe wheels, on the other hand, with their quite intricate design, are a novelty, and even if they may wear the Raging Bull’s corporate logo on the center caps, they are new. They’re called the NL7, and even if Novitec may not mention Vossen’s name in the social media post embedded at the bottom of the page, we know that these two companies have teamed up on countless projects over the years.\nA set of sport springs, which seem to have brought the car’s body a bit closer to the asphalt, are also part of the makeover. And so is the exhaust system, which is said to give the V8 engine a “ more emotional soundtrack .” However, in order to retain the OEM appearance , Novitec decided to keep the original tailpipes. Thus, even if the exhaust system is indeed new, the tips aren’t.\nAnd yes, it is a V8 engine that powers Lamborghini’s new “baby” supercar, in case you forgot, as while its predecessors, the Huracan and Gallardo, had naturally aspirated V10s, the Temerario uses a 4.0-liter mill with twin turbocharging. On top of that, the ICE is assisted by no fewer than three electric motors, because this model is a plug-in hybrid, just like Lambo’s entire modern-day lineup that also comprises the Revuelto and the Urus SE Performante.\nPhoto: Instagram | novitecgroup In total, the Lamborghini Temerario has 907 hp (920 ps/677 kW) to play with, with the ICE kicking out 789 hp (800 ps/588 kW) between 9,000 and 9,750 rpm. The supercar is 0.2 seconds slower than the Revuelto and 0.3 seconds slower than the new Revuelto SV, taking 2.7 seconds to hit 62 mph (100 kph) from a standstill. Keep the right pedal pinned to the floor where the road and legislation allow it, and you will eventually see mph (343 kph) on the digital speedometer behind the steering wheel.\nThis one of the three screens inside the Temerario, which starts at approximately $400,000 for the 2027 model year in the United States of America, alongside the portrait-oriented infotainment system, and the tiny display in front of the passenger.\nWe’ve said it before and we will say it again: Novitec can easily host a tuning course , regardless of the project, and most of its builds prove that less is indeed more in the tuning game. This particular Lamborghini Temerario stands out for all the right reasons, this writer believes, and it deserves its 15 minutes of online fame.\nIt is not the last Temerario that we will cover from this tuner, because the company is currently working on a fully exposed carbon fiber package, said to come later this year. And knowing how Novitec rolls, we can already tell that we will definitely dig that makeover, just like this one. But the question is, do you agree that the pictured Lambo is on the cool side of things? View this post on Instagram A post shared by Novitec Group (@novitecgroup)\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nlamborghini temerario Lamborghini temerario tuning Novitec supercar PHEV v8\nAbout the author: Cristian Gnaticov\nAfter a series of unfortunate events put an end to Cristian's dream of entering a custom built & tuned old-school Dacia into a rally competition, he moved on to drive press cars and write for a living. He's worked for several automotive online journals and now he's back at autoevolution after his first tour in the mid-2000s. Full profile\nUP NEXT 30 New Premium Diecast Collectibles for November of 2026",
